{"name":"Winter Glaze","hex":"#eaebe0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"winter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Traditionally the fourth of the four seasons, typically regarded as spanning either the period between the winter solstice to the spring equinox, or the months of December, January, and February in temperate and polar regions of the Northern Hemisphere and the months of June, July, and August in the Southern Hemisphere. It is the time when the sun is lowest in the sky, resulting in short days, and the time of year with the lowest atmospheric temperatures for the region.","The period of decay, old age, death, or the like."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spend the winter (in a particular place).","To store something (for instance animals) somewhere over winter to protect it from cold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/winter"},{"term":"glaze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The vitreous coating of pottery or porcelain; anything used as a coating or color in glazing.","A transparent or semi-transparent layer of paint."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To install windows.","To apply a thin, transparent layer of coating."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glaze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glaze (ice)","blocks":[{"text":"Glaze or glaze ice, also called glazed frost or verglas, is a smooth, transparent and homogeneous ice coating occurring when freezing rain or drizzle hits a surface. It is similar in appearance to clear ice, which forms from supercooled water droplets. It is a relatively common occurrence in temperate climates in the winter when precipitation forms in warm air aloft and falls into below-freezing temperature at the surface."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Ice_on_grass.jpg/330px-Ice_on_grass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glaze_(ice)"},{"title":"Winter","blocks":[{"text":"Winter is the coldest and darkest season of the year in temperate and polar climates. It occurs after autumn and before spring, when the hemisphere is oriented away from the Sun as a result of the tilt of Earth's axis. Different cultures define different dates as the start of winter, and some use a definition based on weather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Winter_forest_silver.jpg/330px-Winter_forest_silver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winter"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Trees_with_Ice.JPG/500px-Trees_with_Ice.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Trees_with_Ice.JPG/1280px-Trees_with_Ice.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Trees_with_Ice.JPG/500px-Trees_with_Ice.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Glazed tree branches are noted for both their beauty and their tendency to snap","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATrees_with_Ice.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Freezing_Rain_on_Tree_Branch.jpg/500px-Freezing_Rain_on_Tree_Branch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Freezing_Rain_on_Tree_Branch.jpg/1280px-Freezing_Rain_on_Tree_Branch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Freezing_Rain_on_Tree_Branch.jpg/500px-Freezing_Rain_on_Tree_Branch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Branch fully encapsulated in glaze","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/